# IAM (Users, Roles, Policies) ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## 🤯 Problem

Your School ERP's Express server needs to read/write files in S3.

Do you put your ROOT AWS account password

directly inside the server's code?

If that code leaks, someone gets FULL control of your entire AWS account.

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine the School Principal (Root Account)

has the master key to EVERYTHING —

every classroom, every safe, every office.

You'd NEVER hand that master key

to a Teacher who only needs access to their OWN classroom.

Instead, you give the Teacher a LIMITED key (IAM User/Role)

that ONLY opens what they actually need.

---

## ✅ What is it?

IAM (Identity and Access Management) controls

WHO (Users, Roles) can do WHAT (Policies)

on WHICH AWS resources.

Users → represent people or applications.

Roles → temporary permissions, often assumed by AWS services themselves.

Policies → JSON documents defining exactly what's allowed/denied.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

To follow the PRINCIPLE OF LEAST PRIVILEGE —

give each user/service ONLY the exact permissions it needs,

nothing more, drastically limiting damage if something is compromised.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

🔑 The Master Key vs the Classroom Key

Root Account = master key to everything (never use for daily tasks).

IAM User/Role = a limited key that opens ONLY what's needed.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

The EC2 server running Express

is given an IAM ROLE

that allows ONLY `s3:PutObject` and `s3:GetObject`

on the specific "student-photos" S3 bucket —

it CANNOT delete the database, touch billing, or access unrelated services,

even if the server itself gets compromised.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Using the ROOT account for everyday development/deployment tasks.

Best practice: lock away root credentials,

create IAM Users/Roles with SCOPED permissions

for actual day-to-day work.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::student-photos/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Similar to Route Guards (`CanActivate`) restricting

WHICH users can access WHICH routes —

IAM restricts WHICH identities

can perform WHICH actions on WHICH AWS resources.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

IAM (Identity and Access Management) controls authentication and authorization within AWS. IAM Users represent individual people or applications, IAM Roles provide temporary permissions often assumed by AWS services, and Policies are JSON documents that define exactly what actions are allowed or denied on specific resources. Following the principle of least privilege — granting only the minimum permissions needed — is a core AWS security best practice.

---

## 📝 Keywords

IAM

User

Role

Policy

Least Privilege

Root Account

---


# EC2 (Virtual Servers) ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## 🤯 Problem

Your Express + Node.js School ERP backend

needs somewhere to actually RUN, 24/7,

reachable from the internet.

Where does the actual `node app.js` process live?

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine RENTING a fully equipped Computer,

sitting in Amazon's data center,

that you can remotely log into,

install anything on, and run your app on —

instead of buying and physically hosting your own machine.

---

## ✅ What is it?

EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud) provides

VIRTUAL SERVERS ("instances") in the cloud —

you choose the CPU/RAM size, operating system,

and can install/run ANYTHING on it, just like a normal computer.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

To run your backend server (Express, Node.js)

on a machine that's ALWAYS on and internet-accessible.

Full control over the environment — install any software you need.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

💻 The Rented Computer

EC2 = a computer you rent by the hour,

sitting in Amazon's data center,

that you fully control remotely.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

An EC2 instance runs:

`node app.js` (the Express API)

24/7, reachable at a public IP,

so React/Next.js frontends and mobile apps

can send requests to it anytime.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Thinking EC2 automatically scales or manages itself.

EC2 is essentially a RAW virtual machine —

YOU are responsible for patching, scaling, and managing it,

unlike more "managed" services (Elastic Beanstalk, Lambda).

---

## 💻 Pattern

```bash
# SSH into your EC2 instance
ssh -i my-key.pem ec2-user@your-instance-public-ip

# Then, just like any Linux server:
git clone your-repo
npm install
npm start
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Think of EC2 like your OWN development laptop,

just rented, remote, and always powered on —

you install Node, clone your repo, and run your app on it

exactly like you would locally.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud) provides resizable virtual servers in the cloud. You choose the instance's CPU, memory, and operating system, then install and run any software on it, just like a physical computer — commonly used to host backend applications like a Node.js/Express API. Unlike managed services, EC2 gives full control but also full responsibility for maintenance, patching, and scaling.

---

## 📝 Keywords

EC2

Virtual Machine

Instance

SSH

Compute

