# Lambda (Serverless Functions) ⭐⭐⭐⭐☆

## 🤯 Problem

You need a small function that runs

ONLY when a new student photo is uploaded to S3

(to generate a thumbnail).

Running a WHOLE EC2 server 24/7

just for this occasional, tiny task feels wasteful.

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine hiring a Freelancer

who ONLY shows up and gets paid

for the EXACT moment a specific task needs doing

(a new photo arrives),

instead of hiring a full-time employee

sitting idle at a desk all day waiting for that one task.

---

## ✅ What is it?

Lambda lets you run CODE

WITHOUT managing any server at all ("serverless").

Your function runs automatically

in response to a TRIGGER (an S3 upload, an API call, a schedule),

and you're billed ONLY for the exact time it actually runs.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

No server to manage or pay for when idle.

Automatically scales — from zero requests, to thousands, instantly.

Great for small, EVENT-DRIVEN tasks.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

🧑‍💼 The Freelancer

Lambda = paid ONLY for the moment work actually happens,

no idle "employee" sitting around costing money 24/7.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

A Lambda function triggers AUTOMATICALLY

whenever a new file lands in the `student-photos` S3 bucket —

it resizes the image into a thumbnail

and saves it back to S3,

with ZERO dedicated server running in between uploads.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Using Lambda for LONG-RUNNING processes.

Lambda functions have a MAXIMUM execution time (15 minutes)

and aren't ideal for constantly-running servers

(like your main Express API) — better suited for short, event-driven tasks.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```js
exports.handler = async (event) => {
    const uploadedFile = event.Records[0].s3.object.key;
    // resize image logic here
    return { statusCode: 200, body: `Processed ${uploadedFile}` };
};
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Similar to a single, isolated event handler function

(like an `onClick` callback)

that only runs in response to a SPECIFIC trigger,

rather than a whole component sitting mounted and active all the time.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

Lambda is AWS's serverless compute service, letting you run code in response to events — like an S3 file upload, an API request, or a scheduled trigger — without provisioning or managing any servers. You're billed only for the actual execution time, and it automatically scales from zero to many concurrent executions, making it ideal for small, event-driven tasks rather than long-running processes.

---

## 📝 Keywords

Lambda

Serverless

Event-driven

Trigger

Pay-per-execution

